        "verdict": "bug",
        "confidence": 0.72,
        "severity": "low",
        "category": "wrong-result",
        "title": "ordinal() picks the wrong suffix for negative integers (-1 -> '-1th')",
        "reason": "The docstring says ordinal() \"works for any integer\", and negative ints are valid input. The code takes `value % 10` and `value % 100` on the signed value. Python's modulo is non-negative, so -1 gives digit 9 and the suffix 'th', while the suffix for -n should depend on the digits of n. The repro is correct and fails deterministically. The only doubt is that negative ordinals are unusual and untested, and no test or doc promises '-1st'. Even so, '-1th' is clearly wrong, and the fix is a one-line `abs(value)` in the modulo expressions, so a maintainer would likely accept it.",
        "maintainerWouldFix": true
      },
      "status": "confirmed",
      "bugId": "PH-001",
      "reproSource": "import sys, types\nif \"humanize._version\" not in sys.modules:\n    _m = types.ModuleType(\"humanize._version\")\n    _m.__version__ = \"0\"\n    sys.modules[\"humanize._version\"] = _m\nfrom humanize.number import ordinal\n\n\ndef test_repro_ordinal_negative():\n    assert ordinal(-1) == \"-1st\"\n    assert ordinal(-2) == \"-2nd\"\n    assert ordinal(-3) == \"-3rd\"\n    assert ordinal(-21) == \"-21st\"\n",
      "bugDir": ".proofhound/runs/20261017-070558/bugs/PH-001-ordinal-picks-the-wrong-suffix-for-negative-inte"
    },
